WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.910
ANDY CHANG: Hi, and
welcome to SEC 212, where

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.160
we'll cover how you can
take control of security

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.340
with the Security
Command Center.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.630
I'm Andy Cheng, a product
manager in Google's Cloud

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.770
Security team.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.890
And I'm here with my
co-presenter, Kyle Olive.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:19.710
KYLE OLIVE: Hi.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.050
I'm Kyle Olive, a product
manager on the Google Cloud

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.625
Security Command Center team.

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:26.000
ANDY CHANG: Kyle
will be covering

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.417
some of the built-in
vulnerability

00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:30.205
and threat detectors in
detail later in this talk.

00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:31.580
In addition, we'll
be showing you

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.000
an example of how Veolia, a
multinational based in France,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.670
operating utilities, water and
waste management, and energy

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.340
services, has integrated
Security Command Center

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.600
into their security workflow.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.200
Staying in control of
your security is hard.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.940
As cloud adoption grows and
your teams get even more agile,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.290
the accelerated velocity
of cloud development

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.050
means that you have increasing
numbers of dynamic resources

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.860
and services being deployed,
expanding your attack surface

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.170
and potentially expanding
both the risks that you

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.950
have to address and the scale
and complexity of your estate.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.140
Traditional tools
needed also seem

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.390
to endlessly multiply
and make it hard

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.140
for you to drive consistency
and get one unified view

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.220
of your security posture.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.380
As enterprises move
from on-premise

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.620
to public cloud environments,
three common concerns

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.360
are top of mind.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.860
How do I get full visibility?

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.780
And how do I get an
accurate assessment

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.630
of my attack surface,
my security posture,

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.640
and my compliance state?

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.290
Can I continue to
detect and respond

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.610
to the threats that are most
relevant and important to me?

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.310
How do I learn to use and deal
with the additional complexity

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.270
of new architectures, policy
systems, technologies,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.725
and the services available
in public clouds?

00:01:47.725 --> 00:01:50.100
And across all three
of these, how do I

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.290
keep on top of all
of these things

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.750
as the scope of services
and the size of deployments

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.870
from my teams continually
grows and increases?

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.090
Finally, will I need to know how
to manage an ever-growing set

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.690
of security tools with disparate
interfaces and capabilities

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.030
in order to keep ahead
of these challenges?

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.560
Or can I use something
native and built in?

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.470
Security Command Center
provides you a single place

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.460
to control the security of
your estate on Google Cloud.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.390
Security Command Center
offers a unified set

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.720
of capabilities that enables
you consistent and continuous

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.960
organization-wide
visibility, detection,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.350
and enables you to respond
all from one place,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.470
leveraging the best of Google
Cloud Security expertise

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:39.640
with all the services managed
by Google and at Google scale.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.280
Security Command Center Premium
addresses your challenges

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.730
by providing you
with a single place

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.430
for organizational-level
control over the built-in threat

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.640
prevention and threat
detection capabilities offered

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.960
by Google Cloud.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.300
From one unified place,
you can manage and review

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.770
findings related
to your security

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.400
posture, your active threats,
the underlying assets being

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.610
impacted and their metadata,
and the different types

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.430
and classifications of
data potentially at risk,

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.890
all through a Google-managed
system that automatically

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.030
scales and deploys with the
growth of your organization.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.330
Security Command Center offers a
single consistent place for you

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.310
to enable which
security services should

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.290
be enabled where.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.020
By default, all resources
supported by Security Command

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.120
Center are automatically
monitored and covered

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.980
by threat prevention and
detection capabilities.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.740
So as your developers
spin up new projects

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.050
and your organization
grows, these policies

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.120
get applied
automatically, reducing

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.490
the danger of an
inadvertent blind spot

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.160
in your understanding
of your attack surface.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.110
With Security Command
Center Premium,

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.810
you can continuously monitor
your security posture,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.880
monitor your asset
configurations

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.240
against common misconfigurations
and against compliance controls

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.837
like CIS and PCI.

00:03:53.837 --> 00:03:55.670
You can automatically
detect vulnerabilities

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.370
in your public web applications
from the OWASP Top 10.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.640
And in addition, you
can detect and protect

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.370
against active threats through
real time log and cluster

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.780
analysis to find bad actors
and stop them in real time.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:12.050
I'll now turn it over
to Kyle to provide you

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.318
details on each of these
built-in capabilities.

00:04:15.318 --> 00:04:17.110
KYLE OLIVE: Keeping
your deployments secure

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:18.563
is an ongoing challenge.

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:20.480
But as Andy mentioned,
Security Command Center

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.310
includes several
capabilities that

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.530
help you increase your
scope of coverage,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.510
understand your
security posture,

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.518
and enable you to become
more secure over time.

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:31.310
We'll spend a little
bit of time going over

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.290
each of these
capabilities before diving

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.660
into a real life
demonstration of how

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.870
you can use Security Command
Center in your organization.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.000
First, let's talk
about the capabilities

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.310
designed to help you
understand and improve

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.510
your security posture.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.270
Security Health Analytics
monitors and analyzes

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.130
your infrastructure
configurations

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.410
for known insecure
configurations

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.180
and configurations
that are not complying

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.220
with compliance controls.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.650
Security Health Analytics
offers a broad scope

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.040
of coverage out-of-box and
with minimal configuration.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.480
You simply turn
it on for your org

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.120
and get coverage for any
existing or any new projects

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.590
that get spun up.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.330
Our goal with Security
Health Analytics

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.600
is to cover the broader scope
of Google Cloud Services,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.690
leveraging Google security
know-how to provide detections

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.000
and recommendations that
will make customers more

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.020
secure across Storage, Compute,
and several other Google Cloud

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.460
Services.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.170
Every detection
includes the metadata

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.680
you need to triage that finding
and act on it, including

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.530
context-specific
remediation instructions.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.900
Additionally, we have recently
improved Security Health

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.300
Analytics to have an
even tighter integration

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.070
with your resource
configurations,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.440
detecting changes in
configurations in real time

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.590
and allowing you to react and
respond to critical events

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.570
before a breach can occur.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.370
Security Health
Analytics can detect

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.750
some of the most impactful
misconfigurations,

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:52.100
like public buckets
or open firewalls,

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.850
while also providing
more nuanced security

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.760
recommendations around
networking, logging,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.260
and monitoring.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.600
These recommendations come
from our security teams.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.520
And many of them are
the same guidelines that

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.160
Google itself puts into place.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.140
The selection of
detections found here

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.990
are only a small subset of
the total number of detections

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.070
that are available.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:15.380
And that scope of coverage will
continue to increase over time.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.720
One of the most exciting
things we've added this year

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.610
is the ability for
Security Health Analytics

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.090
to perform continuous compliance
monitoring, identifying

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.280
resources that are known
to be misconfigured

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:30.350
against compliance controls from
the CIS GCP benchmark, PCI-DSS,

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:34.940
ISO 27001, NIST
800-53, and more.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.900
These findings are
surfaced through dashboards

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.830
that can be filtered and
scoped by projects or folders,

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.980
giving you control over how
you assess your environment.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.010
These reports can be a starting
point for remediative actions

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.080
or exported out
as a CSV that can

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.250
be used to track your
compliance state over time.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.860
But your security posture
is made up of more than just

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.500
your resource configurations.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.800
It's also important to monitor
against vulnerabilities

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.690
in your web applications.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.090
Security Command Center
includes Web Security Scanner

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.060
for that purpose.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.970
Web Security Scanner
automatically detects and scans

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:09.160
your exposed public
web applications

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.890
for the most common and
impactful vulnerabilities found

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.058
in web apps.

00:07:13.058 --> 00:07:14.600
Like Security Health
Analytics, there

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.465
is minimal configuration
overhead required here.

00:07:17.465 --> 00:07:19.840
You just turn on the service,
and Security Command Center

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.880
will automatically
detect and configure

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:26.650
web application scans for apps
running on GAE, GKE, or GCE.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.960
These scans will crawl your
public web applications

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.990
in order to detect some of the
most severe vulnerabilities

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.720
found in web applications,
including vulnerabilities

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.050
from categories of the OWASP Top
10, like cross-site scripting

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.417
and security misconfigurations.

00:07:40.417 --> 00:07:42.250
These, again, are the
same tools that Google

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:44.595
uses to protect and scan
our own web applications.

00:07:44.595 --> 00:07:45.970
And we're making
these detections

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.883
available to Security
Command Center customers.

00:07:49.883 --> 00:07:51.550
All of the findings
we just talked about

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.490
can be centrally managed from
the vulnerabilities dashboard

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.700
or via a single Security
Command Center API.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.130
Each finding is associated
with the severity

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.140
and other additional
metadata, allowing

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.610
you to assess the potential
impact of the finding

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.440
and triage it for action.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.320
Finally, as mentioned,
each finding

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.220
includes context-specific
remediation instructions

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.150
that you can quickly
act on yourself,

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.055
automate against, or
copy into a ticket

00:08:15.055 --> 00:08:18.250
that you can assign
to a project owner.

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:20.750
But sometimes, despite
all our best efforts,

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:22.700
malicious actors
manage to compromise

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.800
some part of a deployment.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.570
Security Command Center
also provides you

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.580
the tools you need to
detect these intrusions

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.930
and respond to
them in real time.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.450
First up, we have
Event Threat Detection.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:37.120
At Google, we have dedicated
threat intelligence groups

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:39.640
analyzing activities on
the internet for the latest

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.530
threat signals, not only
to protect ourselves,

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:44.215
but also our customers.

00:08:44.215 --> 00:08:46.090
Event Threat Detection
leverages those threat

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:49.240
signals to provide an
enhanced level of detection,

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.400
allowing you to
catch more incidents.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.260
These detections
happen in real time

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.110
as soon as the log is generated,
allowing you to respond

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.897
to incidents in seconds.

00:08:57.897 --> 00:08:59.980
As your logs are generated,
Event Threat Detection

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:02.170
monitors them, analyzing
entries from your audit

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.900
logs, your syslogs,
and your network logs.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:07.300
From there, we apply
our detection logic

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.800
and use Google's
threat intelligence

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.230
to identify malicious
actors performing one

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.710
of several types of activities.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.660
Event Threat Detection
can detect activities

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.570
related to malware or coin
mining running on your systems.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.803
It can detect active data
exfiltration or IAM abuse,

00:09:21.803 --> 00:09:23.970
and will be able to leverage
user behavior analytics

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.890
to detect compromises
on accounts

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.215
and alert you to that activity.

00:09:27.215 --> 00:09:28.590
And the scope of
these detections

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.420
will only increase over time.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.550
Google's expertise can also
be leveraged in other ways,

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:35.820
like with our Container
Threat Detection capabilities

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.510
now in Security Command Center.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.000
Container Threat Detection
is an exciting new capability

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.070
available in Security
Command Center.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.630
At its core, it's a Linux
Security Module baked

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:49.500
into COS and GKE that can detect
incidents occurring on your GKE

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.390
workloads in real time.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.880
Once again, Security
Command Center

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.100
handles the management
of these deployments,

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.340
automatically enabling Container
Threat Detection on clusters

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.610
and projects per your settings
as soon as those clusters get

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.640
spun up, removing the
overhead that regularly comes

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.470
with this kind of protection.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.390
Once enabled, Container
Threat Detection

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.760
monitors real-time
changes on your workloads

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.210
to detect incidents like the
execution of added binaries,

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:16.640
new linked libraries, or
when a cluster suddenly

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.500
opens a reverse shell
to the internet,

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.510
all signs of a compromise.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.580
In just seconds, you
can alert on an incident

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.105
and even take automated
action against it.

00:10:25.105 --> 00:10:26.480
For an example of
that, we'll now

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.460
switch over to a demo
from Guillaume Blaquiere.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:30.132
At Veolia.

00:10:30.132 --> 00:10:32.950
Guillaume will walk us
through how Veolia leverages

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:35.620
Security Command Center to
respond to vulnerabilities

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:37.870
automatically in real
time in a way that

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.700
can be extended to
any of the detections

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.080
we just talked about.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.855
Let's take a look.

00:10:43.855 --> 00:10:45.300
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Hi, everyone.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.300
I'm Guillaume Blaquiere.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.560
I'm [INAUDIBLE] expert
on Cloud Platform.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.270
I'm also a cloud master and
Lead Architect at Veolia.

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.655
Veolia is a major
French company,

00:10:55.655 --> 00:11:00.170
a [INAUDIBLE] company focused
on water, waste, and energy,

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.420
present in 50
countries in the world

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:06.330
and with more than
170,000 employees.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:11.510
Veolia work with more than
seven years with Google Cloud.

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.660
And today we have more
than 4,000 projects

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:18.110
on GCP with internal
and external people

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:21.740
and partners that work on
the platform for building

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:25.650
and solutions in production.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:30.460
Today, managing the security
is a challenge for us,

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:32.470
because we are a
small team in Paris.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.910
And we have a lot of people, a
lot of countries, several time

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.880
zones, several different
affinities with the security.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:43.540
And we have a lot of work to do.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:47.290
For example, when we have
to review the firewalls

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.350
and to see what is
open too widely,

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.770
we perform batches
every three months.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:56.830
And we review all the firewalls.

00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:03.970
We contact the project owner to
review with them if it's normal

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:07.390
or not that you have this
rule and so on and to fix it

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:11.170
or to [INAUDIBLE] these rules?

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.750
It's a lot of work
every three months.

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:15.260
It's not very interesting.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:16.790
It's time consuming.

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.820
And we perform this
only every three months.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:22.060
And in three months, it
can happen a lot of things.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:25.600
Now, with Security
Command Center,

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.260
every event in the cloud are
scanned by the Security Command

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:29.830
Center.

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:34.570
And when the findings is found,
it is published on Pub/Sub.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:38.520
And the message is
consumed by Cloud Run.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.830
In turn, we have implemented
two actions on the message.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.370
The first one is to
publish a message in Slack.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:49.870
The second one is to use the
Google API for immediate action

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.670
on the finding, for
example, to revoke

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.820
some authorization on a bucket.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:58.620
We have in our road map
to implement other things,

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.510
for example, to send an
email on the findings,

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.550
to open a ticket
in our ticketing

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:06.740
tool, servicenow, at Veolia.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:12.580
And we can also store
only the real finding

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:16.750
for Veolia and then to build
a dashboard on these findings.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:20.440
And at the end, we can provide
a global view on the security

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.410
to the security team, to the
managers, to the auditors,

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.220
and so on.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.860
So now I would like
to present to you

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.990
a demo of what we have done
today with the Security Command

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:33.580
Center.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:35.950
And here, I have
the firewall rules.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:39.850
Just go under Findings here.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.880
We are in the landing
page for the findings.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.170
And we can filter by security.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:48.970
But my preferred view is
here, the Findings Changed.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:49.470
Why?

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.150
Because you can see what is new.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:56.395
What are the new findings
in your environment,

00:13:56.395 --> 00:14:00.490
in your context, the last hour,
the last day, the last week,

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:01.020
and so on?

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:05.090
It's very efficient
for working with this.

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:08.730
So now imagine I
have a user that

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:11.020
would like to share
some documents

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.440
with a customer and partners.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.950
There's a lot of
people involved.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:19.510
And the user don't want
to grant all the users one

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:23.830
by one in the console.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:28.510
For this, user want to
make the bucket public

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.285
and to share it widely.

00:14:30.285 --> 00:14:32.410
At Veolia, we don't
want this, because we

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:39.250
consider that the data stored in
our buckets are Veolia's data.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:40.890
And these are confidential data.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:44.320
And we don't want to
share it with anybody.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:47.380
That way, we can
apply for example,

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:50.860
an organization policy
named domain [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.140
that prevents you
to grant all users

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:56.380
and all authenticated
users to the buckets.

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:58.860
But the side effect of
this organization policy

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.060
is you can't grant all users
and all authenticated users

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:06.340
to Cloud Run, [INAUDIBLE],,
and App Engine.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.100
And that way, with this
organization policy,

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:13.720
we can't make public
our API and our website.

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:17.380
Or we can whitelist a project
for making public our API

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.080
and our website
[INAUDIBLE] App Engine.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:23.200
But we also whitelist
our bucket and can't

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:25.450
grant all users and
all authenticated users

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:26.260
to the buckets.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.120
And we don't want this.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:32.090
Now with Security Command Center
and the [INAUDIBLE] findings,

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:33.430
we can do this.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:36.580
For example, here
I have a bucket.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.960
And I would like
to share publicly

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:42.050
this [INAUDIBLE] bucket.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.750
I will add all users here.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:51.710
And I will share all the
objects of my bucket.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.550
When I save, I have
a warning message.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:56.420
And I'll say, now
your bucket is public.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:57.960
And yes, say, OK.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:15:59.790
I'm aware of this.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:04.655
And a finding is found by
Security Command Center.

00:16:04.655 --> 00:16:08.120
A message is published
to the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:13.550
And our [INAUDIBLE] instance
publish a message on Slack.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.290
Hey, you have a finding here.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:21.382
And we removed automatically
the authorization of the bucket.

00:16:21.382 --> 00:16:22.590
We can go back to the bucket.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:25.270
You can see here, it's
publicly accessible.

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:26.460
But in fact not--

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.850
if I refresh the
page, the bucket--

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:32.310
the authorization has
been removed automatically

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.470
by calling the Google API.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:40.590
So now we don't have a problem,
only two or three seconds

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.890
between the public
access and the revocation

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:47.470
by the [INAUDIBLE] instance.

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:50.430
Same thing, for example,
with the firewall rules--

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:53.030
I would like to use
Veolia's resources

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:56.680
in my professional account to
build a [INAUDIBLE] Minecraft

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:00.540
server for playing with
my friends and my family.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:04.150
And I've created a compute
instance, deployed my Minecraft

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:04.650
instance.

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.430
And now I have to
create firewall rules.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:10.040
So I would create
firewall rules.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.440
[INAUDIBLE] here, I can name it.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:15.119
If I'm stupid, I
name it Minecraft.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:16.790
But I'm stupid.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:22.010
And I can allow all the
instances of network.

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:26.180
I open it very widely,
because my friends, my family,

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:27.470
and a lot of people--

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:31.340
and I would like to open
it on the port 10,000,

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:34.964
because my server [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:34.964 --> 00:17:36.980
Now, I will click on Create.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:41.220
And as before, in a few
seconds, I can see here

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:47.240
in the Slack Channel, oh,
you have an open firewalls

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.260
findings.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:51.000
And I don't know what to do.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:54.900
I don't know if it's
normal or not normal.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:56.940
And I can ask the
security team do

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.310
to a review on these findings.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:17:59.810
Why?

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:04.250
Because when you have a bucket
open publicly on the internet,

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.350
you can immediately
download the contents,

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:09.350
and the data leak is instant.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:15.890
When you open a strange
port on firewall rules,

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:21.440
maybe some intruder can use
it and enter into the system.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:22.650
But it's not instant.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:26.390
It can take days, weeks,
or months for this.

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:30.050
And you can take more
time to review the rules.

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:32.390
And now I can see
what's happened.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:33.950
I click on the link.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:37.070
And I can see, oh, I have
a rule named Minecraft

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:39.780
open on internet on this port.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:41.310
No, it's not normal.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.010
I have to delete it.

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:45.470
And now it's--

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:48.680
I can react immediately
on firewalls open.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:52.100
And I don't have to perform
batches every three months

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.370
as before.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.420
Now the platform is more secure.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:59.420
It's quicker to
react on the events.

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:02.900
With the premium version, we
have more and more findings.

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:07.790
And we can fine-tune what we'd
like to allow and to disallow

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:09.140
on our platform.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:12.470
And it's helped us a lot
in our day-to-day job.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.460
It's less boring as before.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.930
So this evolution and this
real-time notification

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:21.080
is very, very great
for us at Veolia.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.957
Thank you.

